---
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<figure class="plate">
  <img src={u("/art/lapidarius-gracisce.webp")}
       alt="A constructed illustration: a stonecutter in shirtsleeves and a leather apron working a block of white limestone with a mallet and a toothed chisel in a bare workshop; stone dust in the light; points, claws, a wooden square and a straight-edge on the wall; a trough, a kerbstone and a carved slab on the floor; through the open door a stepped village lane and a campanile."
       width="784" height="1100" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">
    <strong>A <em>lapidarius</em> of Gračišće. This is not Antonio.</strong> No likeness of Antonius Defranceschi exists
    and none is claimed here. The face in this picture is invented, and because it is invented it is not given his name
    — a made-up face captioned with a real person's name is a forgery, and that rule does not bend for a good painting.
    <br /><br />
    What it is instead is the trade, which the record does describe. The Gračišće Status Animarum of 1870 enters house 19
    as «Antonius Defranceschi, <em>lapidarius</em>» — stonecutter — born 20 September 1825; his father in house 5 was an
    <em>agricola</em>, a farmer, and his son Josephus carried the trade to the coast, where the Crikvenica register of
    1906 calls him «vešti klesar sada tvorničar», a skilled stonemason now a factory worker. A <em>lapidarius</em> is not
    a sculptor. He cut window surrounds, kerbs, troughs and grave slabs out of white Istrian limestone, and the workshop
    was poor. Every one of those things is in the picture, and they are the reason it is here.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<style>
  .plate { margin: 0; }
  .plate img { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 440px; height: auto; border-radius: 2px; }
</style>
